+# Cold review of root uniform-clustering scaling boundary
+
+Target ROOT_DERIVATION.md SHA86cd75f43cb4cd0f70076e9e7682b1f8fac22ed20205d3517214f92ff03d3fad, read after freezing own DERIVATION.md SHA0e93e579bef948dbcbf07020a74140960727fb85f2506ca75a981af877a37484. PASS. Root's original proof preceded the boundary-source alignment discussed with this lane; both versions and timing are preserved by root. No new finite calculation or repository edit was performed for this review.
+
+The0411042 equation16 choice correctly aligns the clustering state with the whole-range neutral GNS state used for gap and static-sector results. Its Section2 convention permits one fixed decay parameter and one uniformly small perturbation norm ball; this supplies common C,mu rather than only pointwise clustering or analyticity. The separately stated0412040 periodic version is not used to identify two potentially differently selected states. Since the scaled onsite kinetic operator is fixed and only u=av enters the dimensionless perturbation, varying a alone does not change these equal-time state bounds.
+
+Fixed lattice support cardinality and radius yield physical separation r/(2ell) minus a constant for convergent distinct anchors. Multiplying the clustering bound by any fixed polynomial in ell^-1 still tends to zero; the stronger o(ell^N) statement for each N follows by taking the exponential-series degree larger than the total polynomial degree. Fixed-range metric conversion changes only a positive decay constant. Identity counterterms do not change connected correlations. The argument is valid for bounded operator sequences with the declared norm bounds, not automatically for unbounded lattice electric fields or arbitrary continuum distributions.
+
+The smearing proof uses the stronger directly stated weighted norm budgets, and correctly bounds each pair before summing. No support-volume exponential for the full macroscopic union is introduced. Positive separation of all two-family anchor pairs is essential. With a fixed separation r for every mesh, the r/ell exponent is valid; with only convergence to separated supports one would use r/(2ell), which is still sufficient. The note's two cases are consistent.
+
+The consequence is a conditional exclusion of a particular separated-point or separated-smearing correlator construction. It does not eliminate contact terms or every continuum limit, and it does not identify the loss of a uniform certificate with a phase transition. Its explicit refusal to identify the LR coefficient with actual speed is correct. No correction found.
+
+Own additional proof contains a compatible concrete adverse to an overbroad reading: at u=0 disjoint actual SU3 plaquette variables have mean0, variance1/18 and independence, so normalized sums admit a nonzero Gaussian contact limit. This is not needed for the root proof but demonstrates why its narrow exclusions are necessary. Own mass/LR ratio supplement is likewise separate; it is not silently attributed to this reviewed root note.
